  "name": "Question 8",
  "problem": "If $cot\u2061x=1$ for some $x$, then find the value of $cot\u2061(x+4\\pi ).$",
  "worked_solution": "The period of $y=cot\u2061x$ is $\\pi .$ Therefore, for integer $n$ we have\n\n$$\ncot\u2061x=cot\u2061(x+n\\pi ).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $n=4$ into the right-hand-side of the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}cot\u2061x=cot\u2061(x+4\\cdot \\pi )=cot\u2061(x+4\\pi ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, if $cot\u2061x=1,$ then $cot\u2061(x+4\\pi )=1.$",
